WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.860
M. Night Shyamalan is back with a new&nbsp;
movie, and it might be his darkest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:09.780
one yet. Do you dare to let it inside? Here's&nbsp;
everything we know about "Knock at the Cabin."

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:14.400
M. Night Shyamalan's new horror film, "Knock&nbsp;
at the Cabin," will break into movie theaters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.560
on February 3, 2023. The director confirmed&nbsp;
the release date with a nine-second clip in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:25.740
October 2022, followed by a poster in November&nbsp;
2022 — one depicting four menacing individuals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:31.000
armed to the teeth, looming outside a tiny&nbsp;
cabin. Shyamalan tweeted along with the poster,

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.320
But who are these spooky strangers,&nbsp;
and what exactly do they want?

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.326
"My name's Leonard. It's nice to meet you, Wen."

00:00:41.334 --> 00:00:44.880
While M. Night Shyamalan usually&nbsp;
generates original material,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.920
he has occasionally adapted other people's&nbsp;
works — a la the infamous "Last Airbender"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.880
and his most recent thriller, "Old."&nbsp;
Similarly, "Knock at the Cabin" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:57.420
based on a novel by Paul G. Tremblay,&nbsp;
"The Cabin at the End of the World."

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:02.820
According to CNBC, the fact that "Cabin" is&nbsp;
based on Tremblay's book was kept secret for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:07.800
an extended period of time, presumably to prevent&nbsp;
giving away the film's premise before Shyamalan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.940
and his producers were ready. Shyamalan and&nbsp;
the film's marketing have still shied away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:16.260
from revealing certain elements of the story,&nbsp;
yet have shared an official logline for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:21.180
plot and a trailer that gives us a taste&nbsp;
of the mayhem to come. The logline reads,

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.200
And this is exactly what we see in the trailer&nbsp;
released by distributor Universal Pictures on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.660
September 22. However, there is one huge&nbsp;
question that looms over everything — are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:48.300
these four people just crazy, sadistic torturers?&nbsp;
Or do they truly possess advanced knowledge of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:53.601
coming apocalypse? And if so, are they right to&nbsp;
force this family to make the ultimate sacrifice?

00:01:53.601 --> 00:01:57.406
"If you fail to choose, the world will end."

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.680
Without a doubt, the most famous actor&nbsp;
involved with "Knock at the Cabin" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.120
Dave Bautista of "Guardians of the Galaxy" fame.&nbsp;
The wrestler turned actor will star as Leonard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.960
the hulking yet quiet leader of the&nbsp;
group that's forcing a family to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.160
a horrible choice. Speaking to The Hollywood&nbsp;
Reporter about their upcoming collaboration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.300
M. Night Shyamalan recounted how Bautista's&nbsp;
encounter with Ryan Gosling's character in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:22.260
"Blade Runner 2049" is what drew him&nbsp;
to the actor. Shyamalan explained,

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.240
The remaining three members of Bautista's&nbsp;
group will be rounded out by Rupert Grint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:44.100
Nikki Amuka-Bird, and Abby Quinn. Grint, of&nbsp;
course, is most famous for playing Ron Weasley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:48.600
in the "Harry Potter" franchise. He's also the&nbsp;
star of Shyamalan's Apple TV+ series "Servant."

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:53.520
Amuka-Bird also has previous Shyamalan experience,&nbsp;
starring in the director's aging horror flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.260
"Old," as well as projects like "Jupiter&nbsp;
Ascending," "The Outfit," and "Avenue 5." As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:03.180
for Quinn, she's appeared in TV shows like "Better&nbsp;
Call Saul" and movies like 2019's "Little Women."

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:07.500
So who will be playing the poor&nbsp;
family beset by armed cultists? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:12.120
Jonathan Groff of "Mindhunter," "Hamilton," and&nbsp;
"The Matrix Resurrections" will be starring as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.320
Andrew. He'll be taking a trip to the titular&nbsp;
cabin with Ben Aldridge of "Pennyworth" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.040
"Fleabag," who will be playing Andrew's&nbsp;
husband, Eric. Their young daughter, Wen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.760
will be played by Kristen Cui. This&nbsp;
will be Cui's first acting project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.440
and as Shyamalan is particularly skilled with&nbsp;
getting great performances out of young actors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.040
we're sure the young performer&nbsp;
will absolutely steal the show.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:37.401
M. Night Shyamalan has had quite the&nbsp;
interesting run over the past few years.

00:03:37.401 --> 00:03:44.134
"What was your mistake!? Trusting me? Depending&nbsp;
on me, thinking that I could do this?"

00:03:44.134 --> 00:03:48.892
Coming off a stream of serious bombs, the&nbsp;
filmmaker resuscitated his career with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.892 --> 00:03:52.920
sleeper hits like "Split" and "The Visit,"&nbsp;
while also offering more controversial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:57.420
titles like "Glass" and "Old." Now, he's&nbsp;
bringing his unique sensibilities to "Knock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:01.740
at the Cabin," and he seems incredibly&nbsp;
excited about venturing down this new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.400
twisted path. Speaking with Total&nbsp;
Film, Shyamalan said about the movie,

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:18.660
So expect this home invasion thriller&nbsp;
to go in some strange directions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:24.120
as Shyamalan always has a few dastardly&nbsp;
tricks up his proverbial sleeve. Generally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.260
M. Night Shyamalan writes all his scripts&nbsp;
by himself, putting his unique stamp on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:33.420
screenplays for movies like "Split," "The Visit,"&nbsp;
and "The Sixth Sense." And yes, he did indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:37.680
work on "Knock at the Cabin," adding his own&nbsp;
signature style and tightening things, getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:42.660
the script down to less than 100 pages. However,&nbsp;
while Shyamalan rewrote the script, he wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:47.520
the one who originally penned the screenplay. The&nbsp;
first version of "Knock at the Cabin" was written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.980
by the duo of Steve Desmond and Michael Sherman.&nbsp;
Their script landed on the 2019 Black List, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:57.900
collection of the most talked-about unmade movies&nbsp;
in Hollywood, before making its way to Shyamalan.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:04.140
While M. Night Shyamalan is a master at creating&nbsp;
horror, the filmmaker generally makes movies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:09.240
can be appreciated by a wide range of ages.&nbsp;
Movies like "The Sixth Sense," "Unbreakable,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:15.540
"Signs," and "Split" are all rated PG-13. Up until&nbsp;
now, Shyamalan has only had one film that's rated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:20.880
R — "The Happening." It's a film about plants&nbsp;
causing people to commit suicide — and the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:25.020
it earned the big bad R played a pretty&nbsp;
sizable factor in the film's marketing.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:30.120
However, "The Happening" needs to move over&nbsp;
and make way, as it's no longer Shyamalan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:35.220
only R-rated movie. "Knock at the Cabin" now&nbsp;
also boasts an R rating, for "violence and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:39.360
language," according to MovieWeb. If you've&nbsp;
read Paul G. Tremblay's source material,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:44.880
this should come as no surprise. "The Cabin at&nbsp;
the End of the World" is an incredibly dark tale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:48.960
and while it's not as gory as a&nbsp;
slasher, the violence and torture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:54.300
is disturbing and hits incredibly hard&nbsp;
due to the emotions and stakes at play.

